From 78429a946f4981bb73f3c7494cd7ab430082f5ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 10:13:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): scan line ends with inline SSE2 on x86-64

Comparison variant of #431 for a paired measurement: the whole x86-64
line-end scan is inline, as the NEON scan is on aarch64, so no input of
16 bytes or more makes a call.

`line_end` on x86-64 steps 16 bytes at a time with SSE2 (`pcmpeqb`
against `\n` and `\r`, `por`, `pmovmskb`) and ends on one overlapping
load of the last vector with the lanes before the cursor masked off,
which also serves starts in the last 15 bytes. Documents shorter than
16 bytes take the word scan out of line. SSE2 is part of the x86-64
baseline, so nothing is detected at run time; aarch64 and other
targets are unchanged.

x86-64 release build (fat LTO, target-cpu x86-64) against main: the
parser grows by 2,649 instructions (`parse_document` +889, `parse_list`
+478, `probe_line_inner` +366, `parse_table` +359, `html_block_bounds`
+255, `parse_paragraph` +165), and `line_at`, `block_span`,
`block_end_past` and `skip_line_comments_from` are inlined into their
callers. One site in `probe_line_inner` keeps the scan out of line, as
main keeps its word scan there.

Tests compare every scan with the definition from every start: LF, CR
and CRLF at every offset of every length up to 200, terminator-only
inputs, every byte value in every lane on four backgrounds including
non-ASCII bytes, UTF-8 lines, and terminators around the 16- and
32-byte step edges and just before `from` for every length up to 96.
On x86-64 the SSE2 scan and the short-document word scan are also
checked on their own from every start.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
